// =====================================================================
// Solution C - fiscal year on the date dimension, full version.
//
// Oct-Sep fiscal year, done the recommended way: the date dimension
// carries fiscal columns, and everything else is ordinary filtering and
// grouping on a real field. This is Solution D taken further - a second
// date role, a sortable fiscal-period axis, and KPIs pinned past the
// dashboard filter.
//
// Self-contained: this dataset, the models beside it and the page
// beside it are used by no other solution folder, and this folder uses
// nothing from theirs. The only shared objects are the core-demo base
// models (`dim_dates`, `ecommerce_*`), which every solution extends and
// none of them edits.
//

// The fiscal logic lives in exactly one place, `seed_c_dates`. No
// parameter, no case() mapping, no per-metric date arithmetic, and
// nothing at all on the fact tables. Metrics here are just metrics.
//
// Built on the shared ecommerce models (read-only reference, nothing in
// `01 demo ecommerce/` is modified). `ecommerce_orders.created_date`
// stands in for Seed's close date — repoint the relationship below to
// port this to their tenant.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// What it costs to extend this to more of Seed's data
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
//   a new metric            nothing. Any metric on a joined fact is
//                           fiscal-aware the moment the join exists.
//   a new fact entity       one relationship line here, IF the fact
//                           already exposes a date-typed column.
//                           Otherwise one date dimension on the fact
//                           plus the line — and see the boundary note.
//   a new timestamp field   one role model (three lines, see
//                           `seed_c_dates_signup`) plus one line here.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// The join key is the one place a boundary bug can hide
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// `date_key` is a DATE, so the fact side has to join on a date too.
// `created_date` is already `{{ #SOURCE.created_at }}::date`, cast in
// the warehouse, which is correct. Seed's HubSpot `closedate` is a
// timestamp, so porting means adding an equivalent cast — and it must be
// a warehouse-side cast, not a Holistics date truncation, or the
// tenant-timezone conversion will move deals across the 30 Sep / 1 Oct
// line into the wrong fiscal year. Test those two days explicitly when
// porting.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// Why this shape and not the earlier ones
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// Two earlier attempts pushed the fiscal logic into the metrics: a
// case() mapping each FY to a literal range, then a year-number
// parameter resolved into date bounds. Both worked, both were clunky,
// and both were filter-only — you could not put fiscal year on a chart
// axis or let the AI group by it, because there was no field to group.
//
// A column on the date dimension gives all of that for free, and it is
// also what Chukwudi already told the customer on the call: "we're just
// going to route it through the date dimension model, which means it
// can adapt to any field it needs to adapt".
// =====================================================================
Dataset seed_c {
  label: 'Seed Company - Solution C (Date Dimension, Full)'
  description: '''
Oct-Sep fiscal year carried as columns on the date dimension. Nothing on the fact tables.

- `Fiscal Year` - 2026 means 1 Oct 2025 to 30 Sep 2026. Filter it, group by it, drill it.
- `Fiscal Year Label` - 'FY26' for display.
- `Fiscal Quarter` - 'Q1 (Oct-Dec)'.
- `Fiscal Period Number` - Oct = 1, Sep = 12. Sort key for lining two fiscal years up on one axis.
- `Fiscal Period` - '01 Oct' ... '12 Sep'. Real calendar months, but sorts in fiscal order, so it works directly as a chart axis.
- `Fiscal Year Offset` - 0 = the fiscal year containing today, -1 = the one before.

Chart axes stay on the real calendar date, so month labels are the real months and no
mental translation is needed. Fiscal year is a filter or a grouping, not a shifted date.

`Fiscal Year Offset` resolves against CURRENT_DATE row by row and rolls over on 1 October
with nothing to edit. Two separate things have to be true for a "current FY" card to hold
when the dashboard is filtered to another year: the offset must not re-base (it does not),
and the metric must opt out of the dashboard's date filter. `GMV (Current FY)` does both -
see its definition.

`Signup Date` is the same date dimension in a second role, joined on user signup instead
of order date — the pattern for Seed's close-date-vs-created-date case. `Users Signed Up
(Current FY)` is the demo-safe metric built on it; `Orders from Users Who Signed Up This FY`
is the same idea on the orders fact, and is left off the dashboards because this warehouse's
synthetic signup dates make it collide with `Total Orders` — see the comment on the metric.
'''
  data_source_name: 'demodb'

  models: [
    ecommerce_orders,
    ecommerce_order_items,
    ecommerce_products,
    ecommerce_users,
    ecommerce_cities,
    ecommerce_countries,
    seed_c_dates,
    seed_c_dates_signup,
  ]

  relationships: [
    relationship(ecommerce_order_items.order_id > ecommerce_orders.id, true),
    relationship(ecommerce_order_items.product_id > ecommerce_products.id, true),
    relationship(ecommerce_orders.user_id > ecommerce_users.id, true),
    relationship(ecommerce_users.city_id > ecommerce_cities.id, true),
    relationship(ecommerce_cities.country_code > ecommerce_countries.code, true),

    // Seed's close date equivalent. Repoint this one line to port.
    relationship(ecommerce_orders.created_date > seed_c_dates.date_key, true),

    // The second date role. Both are active: the one-active rule is per
    // model PAIR, and these are two different date models.
    relationship(ecommerce_users.sign_up_date > seed_c_dates_signup.date_key, true),
  ]

  // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  // METRICS — no fiscal logic needed, the date dimension handles it
  // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  metric gmv {
    label: 'GMV'
    type: 'number'
    description: 'Gross value of items ordered, before discount, all statuses.'
    definition: @aql ecommerce_order_items | sum(ecommerce_order_items.quantity * ecommerce_products.price) ;;
    format: '#,###0'
  }

  metric total_orders {
    label: 'Total Orders'
    type: 'number'
    description: 'Count of orders, all statuses.'
    definition: @aql count(ecommerce_orders.id) ;;
    format: '#,###0'
  }

  // The only fiscal-aware metrics needed: cards that must always show
  // the live year regardless of what the dashboard filter is set to.
  //
  // Two parts, and both are load-bearing:
  //   where(...offset == 0)   picks the fiscal year containing today.
  //   of_all(seed_c_dates)  drops any filter the dashboard put on the
  //                           date dimension, so the card does not get
  //                           intersected down to nothing.
  //
  // ORDER MATTERS. `where(...) | of_all(...)` is correct. The reverse,
  // `of_all(...) | where(...)`, silently returns the grand total across
  // every fiscal year — measured under a FY2024 filter: correct form
  // 7,365,785 (= FY2026), reversed form 11,581,542 (= all years).
  //
  // This makes the pinned card work in pure AML. Unmapping the filter
  // from the block in the dashboard UI is no longer required.
  metric gmv_current_fy {
    label: 'GMV (Current FY)'
    type: 'number'
    description: 'GMV for the fiscal year containing today. Holds even when the dashboard is filtered to another fiscal year. Never needs editing.'
    definition: @aql gmv | where(seed_c_dates.fiscal_year_offset == 0) | of_all(seed_c_dates) ;;
    format: '#,###0'
  }

  metric gmv_prior_fy {
    label: 'GMV (Prior FY)'
    type: 'number'
    description: 'GMV for the fiscal year before the one containing today. Pinned the same way as GMV (Current FY).'
    definition: @aql gmv | where(seed_c_dates.fiscal_year_offset == -1) | of_all(seed_c_dates) ;;
    format: '#,###0'
  }

  // Proof that the second role is a real, independent fiscal calendar
  // and not a copy of the first: this one groups and filters on the
  // SIGNUP date's fiscal year, on the same orders fact.
  //
  // NOT ON A DASHBOARD, on purpose. In this demo warehouse the synthetic
  // sign_up_date values are not chronologically consistent with the
  // order dates, so every pre-FY26 order resolves to an FY26 signup.
  // Measured against `total_orders` under each filter: FY23 120/120,
  // FY24 2,776/2,776, FY25 9,172/9,172 — identical. Only FY26 (20,942
  // vs 18,736) and unfiltered (33,010 vs 30,804) differ. Shown next to
  // `Total Orders` it reads as a broken card rather than as a second
  // calendar. The model is correct; the generated data is not. Kept
  // here because it is the right definition and will separate cleanly
  // on Seed's real data.
  metric orders_from_users_signed_up_this_fy {
    label: 'Orders from Users Who Signed Up This FY'
    type: 'number'
    description: 'Orders placed by users whose SIGNUP date falls in the current fiscal year. Uses the second date role, not the order date. See the comment above before putting this on a demo page.'
    definition: @aql count(ecommerce_orders.id) | where(seed_c_dates_signup.fiscal_year_offset == 0) ;;
    format: '#,###0'
  }

  // The demo-safe version of the above. Counts USERS by their own signup
  // fiscal year, which is the one thing in this warehouse the synthetic
  // data gets right (FY23 321, FY24 1,188, FY25 2,336, FY26 6,419 — a
  // sane monotonic ramp). `of_all` pins it past the order-date filter,
  // so it demonstrates both halves at once: a second, independent fiscal
  // calendar, and a card the dropdown genuinely cannot move.
  metric users_signed_up_current_fy {
    label: 'Users Signed Up (Current FY)'
    type: 'number'
    description: 'Users whose SIGNUP date falls in the fiscal year containing today. Second date role, and pinned past the order-date filter.'
    definition: @aql count(ecommerce_users.id) | where(seed_c_dates_signup.fiscal_year_offset == 0) | of_all(seed_c_dates) ;;
    format: '#,###0'
  }
}
